            <div xml:id="d73-a2">

                <pb facs="0178" n="168" sameAs="frus:frus1864p1/pg168"/>

                <opener><dateline rendition="#center">[From the Times of <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1864-02-10">February 10, 1864</date>.]</dateline></opener>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-missing-head-in-printed-volume">[Untitled]</head>
                <p rendition="#center">COMMONS, FEBRUARY 9, 1864.</p>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#italic">The Alexandra and Alabama.</hi></p>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peacocke</hi> asked the under secretary of state
                    for foreign affairs whether the government would lay upon the table of the house
                    copies of their correspondence with the government of the United States, and
                    more especially that portion of it relating to the case of the Alexandria, and
                    the claim by the United States government for compensation for the losses
                    inflicted by the Alabama and other confederate cruisers.</p>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Layard</hi> said that it would not be regular to
                    present papers referring to a case which was still under the consideration of
                    our courts. The papers relating to the Alabama had already been produced. If any
                    others had been received since, he was not aware that there would be any
                    objection to lay them upon the table.</p>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peacocke</hi> asked the honorable gentleman
                    whether he would lay on the table the answer of her Majesty’s government to the
                    despatch of Mr. Seward, relating to the seizure of the Alexandria, which had
                    already.appeared in the newspapers.</p>
                <p>Mr. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Layard</hi> said that he was advised that it
                    would not be right to produce any papers relating to a case which was still
                    under judicial inquiry. [Oh, oh!]</p>
            </div>
